# 35. Multithreading and Multiprocessing

These techniques help run multiple tasks concurrently or in parallel.

## Multithreading

Useful for I/O-bound tasks, such as downloading data or waiting for API responses.

```python
import threading

def task():
    print("Task running")

thread = threading.Thread(target=task)

thread.start()
thread.join()
```

## Multiprocessing

Uses separate processes and can execute CPU-intensive work in parallel.

```python
from multiprocessing import Process

def task():
    print("Processing data")

if __name__ == "__main__":
    process = Process(target=task)

    process.start()
    process.join()
```

## Difference

| Multithreading | Multiprocessing |
|---|---|
| Uses multiple threads | Uses multiple processes |
| Shared process memory | Separate process memory |
| Useful for I/O-bound tasks | Useful for CPU-bound tasks |
| Lower process creation overhead | Higher process creation overhead |

CPython's Global Interpreter Lock affects CPU-bound Python threads in standard builds. Exact behavior depends on Python implementation and build.

